    "available_at": "2026-10-15T17:58:40.369320+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nFirst Sneak Peek at the Porsche Mission S Just Before You're Supposed To See It\n7 photos\nPhoto: kingofthecrest | Instagram\nFootage of Porsche’s highly anticipated Mission S supercar has leaked online just hours before its scheduled world premiere in New York. The video captured by onlookers during an official photo shoot shows it as those around it remove the tarp, exposing a radical new design that breaks away from anything you have seen from Porsche production cars over the years.\nThe concept car serves as the star of Porsche’s new Sportwagenschmiede 2035 , the plan that shifts the automaker's core philosophy from chasing sales volume to prioritizing high-margin, ultra-luxury exclusivity in order to restore brand profitability.\nThe leaked footage captured from a high point reveals the muscular rear end and the low-slung, mid-engined silhouette, inspired by classic Porsche racing heritage. The biggest surprise, which caused massive stir online, is the top-mounted cooling fan integrated into the rear decklid, which is a direct nod to the legendary Porsche 917 air-cooled race cars.\nThe Mission S features prominent rear fenders that envelop the tail, paired with an integrated hoop-style rear spoiler, which reminds Porsche enthusiasts of the iconic 959. The twin-turbocharged supercar manufactured by Porsche from 1986 to 1993 was originally developed as an ultra-high-tech Group B rally car, before it was adapted into a road-legal production model to meet racing requirements.\nPhoto: kingofthecrest | Instagram The roof features Bugatti Chiron-like split glass panels, leading down to an aggressive high-downforce setup, which integrates a massive rear diffuser and stacked quad exhausts. Porsche has been quite secretive about the powertrain of its Mission S , but no, it is not an EV.\nAll speculation points to an all-new, in-house developed 5.0-liter flat-eight engine. The bespoke boxer power plant is rumored to rev up to 8,000 rpm and is expected to be integrated into a plug-in hybrid setup, which means there will be at least one electric motor to assist it.\nRumors have it that the setup will push out between 700 and 750 horsepower (710 to 760 metric horsepower) for a run from 0 to 60 mph (0 to 97 kph) in 2.8 seconds and a top speed in excess of 200 mph (322 kph).\nRight now, Porsche's quickest car is the Taycan Turbo S with 1,019 horsepower (1,034 metric horsepower) and a 0 to 60 time in a neck-snapping 1.9 seconds. The production version of the Porsche Mission S will serve as the spiritual successor to the Carrera GT and the 918 Spyder.\nPhoto: kingofthecrest | Instagram The cabin of the Mission S has also been a top secret at Porsche these past few months, but the car is expected to get a classic, Carrera GT-inspired instrument cluster and at least three distinct driving modes: Normal, Sport, and Track.\nThe Porsche Mission S will make its official debut later today, at 4:30 PM Eastern Time, in New York. That is when we will get all the official numbers. View this post on Instagram A post shared by \"Porsche Content By General Manager Al H” (@kingofthecrest)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPorsche Mission S Porsche Porsche Mission S leaked Porsche Mission S engine Porsche Mission S horsepower\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT This BMW M4 Coupe Could Sell for Cheap at No Reserve",
